      "message": "Sorry to throw a wrench in everything, but I was looking at this change more and I have a potentially different suggestion to the existing approach given that Brandon recently added a `TextureView` cache and made the `Texture` externally ref-counted:\n\nFirst off, this change seems to be mainly aiming at just making the label more clear to users, but for what it\u0027s worth, the labels are a best effort thing and with the new cache, is effectively order dependent w.r.t what a user could end up seeing since we (correctly) don\u0027t use the label as a part of the cache key for the `TextureView`s. Example:\n```\nTextureViewDescriptor desc; // Note that this is the default descriptor.\ndesc.label \u003d \"first view\";\nauto view1 \u003d texture.CreateView(desc);\n\ndesc.label \u003d \"second view\";\nauto view2 \u003d texture.CreateView(desc);\n\nauto view3 \u003d texture.CreateView(nullptr);\n\n// view2 and view 3 will both be displayed with the label \"first view\"\n// even though we set the label differently.\n```\n\nThat said, I\u0027m thinking that for this change, we could just set the label of the descriptor at [1] appropriately if the `originalDesc \u003d\u003d nullptr`. That would mean we don\u0027t need the new extension chain at all. I think to get the same result we would use `\" defaulted from \" + GetTexture()-\u003eFormatLabel(s)` or w.e. the equivalent.\n\nFurthermore (maybe in a follow-up change), I think that the `TextureBase` class could actually have a member named `Ref\u003cTextureView\u003e mDefaultView` that can be lazily created when `CreateView(nullptr)` is called, and we could fast-track to returning it whenever it happens. Then in places where we null out `mTextureViewCache` currently, we would also null out `mDefaultView`. We would want it to be lazily created so that if the user creates a view with the default descriptor but with an explicit label, that the label they used would be the one that sticks.",
